e like him soon do come back  with the state of art knowledge and with  the willingness to live  and build independent ukraine  and we are standing here for this i'm  sure speaking via video link zelinski  lamented how much things had changed  since his visit to toronto in 2019. the  year he said the toronto raptors  surprised everyone with a championship  victory just as ukraine is surprising  much of the world now this is when you  win  when no one is expecting you to win  by bringing together and uniting you do  things that seem to be impossible  canada's deputy prime minister christia  freeland also watched the president's  address with students at the university  of toronto we heard president zielinski  say that what ukraine needs is money it  needs weapons  and it needs ever stronger sanctions  against russia  we agree and my question is after  speaking the president took questions  from canadian students ukraine exchange  student katarina nekoumiaj says hearing  directly from zelinski meant a lot i  felt really proud  proud for our people for our president i  felt really grateful to everyone who  helps ukraine and supports it thank you  very much a rare opportunity to speak  with a wartime president these students  aren't likely to forget  heather urex west global news calgary  [Applause]  still ahead what's on trudeau's agenda  for the commonwealth summit in rwanda  you're watching global national  [Music]  the federal government is freezing  funding for hockey canada over its  handling of sexual assault allegations  the decision to cut the funding comes  just two days after hockey canada  executives testified on parliament hill  about the organization's handling of a  sexual assault complaint involving  members of the 2018 world junior men's  team in the last two years hockey canada  received 14 million dollars from the  federal government  the trudeau government says canada will  open an embassy in the rwandan capital  of kigali the first stop in a trio of  international summits for the prime  minister his government says it hopes to  strengthen diplomatic relations in the  region our chief political correspondent  david aiken is traveling with the prime  minister david what can we expect on  this international trip  well this is the first of three summits  the prime minister will engage in over  the next 10 days with all the crises in  the world they are all important  here in kigali justin trudeau will have  face-to-face meetings with many of the  commonwealth leaders who have yet to  denounce russia's invasion of ukraine uh  the prime minister's officials telling  us that the prime minister will have  some quote frank conversations to  connect the dots between russia's  invasion food shortages supply chain  issues and inflation  well i think that's why it's important  to be here  to hear their perspective to engage it  may be tough to find consensus india for  example has yet to condemn russia's  invasion of ukraine and in fact india's  prime minister narendra modi he is not  at this summit officials in fact worry  that there may not even be a mention of  ukraine in the summit's final communique  now there's 54 members of the  commonwealth but 32 are small island  states in the pacific and caribbean  their top issue climate change it is  literally an existential issue for many  of these states some of which could  literally be underwater within decades  they are looking for wealthy countries  like canada like the uk to help with  financing they need to adapt to rising  sea levels and deal with ever more  destructive hurricanes and typhoons  finally justin trudeau and other leaders  have been put on notice by some activist  groups like amnesty international to  confront the human rights record of  their host rwanda prime minister paul  kagame kagame's government has been  accused of illegally jailing its critics  torturing some critics and even engaging  in some extrajudicial killings now  officials from the pmo say that trudeau  will not hesitate to bring these matters  up when trudeau and kagami meet later  this week nitu david aiken in rwanda  thanks david  next the high flying hopes for a  montreal teen trailblazer at the nba  draft  the nba draft is tomorrow night and one  of the most highly touted prospects is a  canadian nineteen-year-old benedict  matheran from montreal seems destined  for stardom with his smooth shots and  thunderous dunks he's the latest in what  some are calling the golden age of  canadian basketball dan spector has more  on matheran's talent with the ball and  how he's shown strength off the court  he may not be a household name yet but a  whole lot more canadians are about to  get acquainted with 19 year old benedict  matsuring better known south of the  border as ben matherin the montreal  native regularly made crowds go wild  while starring for the university of  arizona for the past two seasons  thursday night matheran is a lock to be  picked high in the nba draft  top six  at the very least  former canadian national team member  dwight walton says matheran will make an  instant impact in the nba his metrics  are off the chart  he is nba  ready now but long before he was  electrifying packed stadiums in college  matheran played on this court in  montreal north one of the city's poorest  neighborhoods he followed his older  sister jennifer an elite basketball  player in her own right i'm just really  proud the game helped jen and ben cope  with the loss of their brother who died  at 15 in a bicycle accident just under a  decade ago matheran choosing to work  even harder to honor his fallen sibling  i think it speaks volume of his  character and his resilience and his  perseverance now ben is set to make the  ultimate leap as more and more canadians  have been doing in recent years  without question it's a golden age  ontario-born nba stars like andrew  wiggins and jamal murray are among those  cementing canada's reputation as a  basketball powerhouse but quebec has  been catching up with chris boucher  logan stort and now matheran all coming  out of montreal north i think that's  going to just catapult basketball more  and give kids more of a chance matheran  says he hopes his story convinces other  kids to believe in themselves even when  nobody else does dan specter global news  montreal  and that is global national for this  wednesday night i'm nitu garcia  tonight's your canada is this rainbow  over toronto we love to see your  candidate please email it to viewers at  globalnational.com  thanks for watching bar nasser will be  at the anchor desk tomorrow have a great  night  [Music]  you
 

